In Cytomulate, we ensure that the collection of “parent-children” relationships form a polytree (a directed acyclic graph whose underlying undirected graph is a tree) or a polyforest (a collection of nonoverlapping polytrees) [
58
].
To accomplish this, a weighted complete undirected graph with cell types as nodes is constructed. In the Creation Mode, the weights on the edges are randomly assigned. In the Emulation Mode, the L2 distances among observed average protein expressions of all cell types are used as weights.
Next, we carve out a spanning tree or a spanning forest via the Clauset-Newman-Moore greedy modularity maximization algorithm [
60
,
61
] followed by Kruskal’s algorithm [
59
]. Finally, to convert the graph to a directed acyclic graph (DAG), a depth-first search with a randomly selected node as the root on each graph component is performed.
